\label{ch:deoptimization}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:deopt-intro}

Speculative optimization---compiling code based on assumptions about runtime behavior---is only viable if the compiler can safely recover when those assumptions are violated. This recovery mechanism is \emph{deoptimization}: the process of abandoning optimized code and reconstructing the execution state in a less-optimized (or interpreted) form. Closely related is \emph{on-stack replacement} (OSR), which allows execution to transition into optimized code while a method is running, and to transition back out.

Together, deoptimization and OSR form the bidirectional bridge between optimized and unoptimized execution. They are the safety net that makes speculative optimization possible, and their correctness and efficiency are critical to the overall performance of modern JIT compilers.

\section{Deoptimization: Concept and Necessity}\label{sec:deopt-concept}

\subsection{Why Deoptimization Is Needed}\label{sec:deopt-need}

Speculative optimizations make assumptions that may be violated at runtime:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A type guard fails: the object is not the expected type.
  \item A class hierarchy assumption is violated: a new subclass is loaded.
  \item A null check fails: a reference assumed non-null is null.
  \item An arithmetic assumption fails: an overflow occurs.
  \item A loop bound assumption fails: the loop iterates more times than expected.
\end{itemize}

When any of these occur, the optimized code can no longer execute correctly. It must transfer control to code that can handle the general case.

\begin{definition}[Deoptimization]\label{def:deoptimization}
\emph{Deoptimization} is the process of:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Detecting that a speculative assumption has been violated.
  \item Abandoning the current optimized execution frame.
  \item Reconstructing the equivalent frame(s) in the unoptimized code.
  \item Transferring control to the unoptimized code at the appropriate point.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Correctness Requirement}\label{sec:correctness}

Deoptimization must preserve the observable behavior of the program. Specifically:

\begin{theorem}[Deoptimization Correctness]\label{thm:deopt-correctness}
After deoptimization, the program must behave as if the speculative optimization had never been applied. All side effects performed before the deoptimization point must be preserved, and all subsequent execution must proceed as if the unoptimized code had been running all along.
\end{theorem}

This requirement constrains both where guards can be placed and how frame reconstruction is performed.

\section{Frame Reconstruction}\label{sec:frame-reconstruction}

\subsection{The Reconstruction Problem}\label{sec:reconstruction-problem}

The optimized code's frame contains values in registers and stack slots that correspond to the unoptimized code's local variables, operand stack entries, and temporary values. The reconstruction problem is to map the optimized state back to the unoptimized state.

\begin{definition}[Frame Reconstruction]\label{def:frame-reconstruction}
\emph{Frame reconstruction} is the process of creating a new activation frame for the unoptimized code (interpreter or baseline compiler) that contains the same logical state as the optimized frame being abandoned.
\end{definition}

\subsection{State Mapping}\label{sec:state-mapping}

The mapping from optimized state to unoptimized state is described by \emph{deoptimization metadata}:

\begin{definition}[Deoptimization Metadata]\label{def:deopt-metadata}
\emph{Deoptimization metadata} (or \emph{deopt descriptor}) is data generated by the compiler for each potential deoptimization point, describing:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The bytecode index (or source location) of the deoptimization point.
  \item The mapping from each local variable and stack slot to its location in the optimized frame (register, stack slot, or constant).
  \item The set of locks held (for languages with synchronized blocks).
  \item The expression stack state (for stack-based virtual machines).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Deoptimization Metadata]\label{ex:deopt-metadata}
For a Java method with a deoptimization point at bytecode index 15:
\begin{verbatim}
  DeoptInfo at bci=15:
    local[0] = RAX (this)
    local[1] = [RBP - 16] (int x)
    local[2] = [RBP - 24] (Object[] arr)
    stack[0] = RCX (result of array load)
    stack[1] = #42 (constant)
    monitors: none
\end{verbatim}
This tells the deoptimization runtime how to fill in the interpreter frame for each local variable and stack entry.
\end{example}

\subsection{Reconstruction Algorithm}\label{sec:reconstruction-alg}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Frame Reconstruction}
\label{alg:frame-reconstruction}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{Deoptimize}{$\text{optimized\_frame}, \text{deopt\_info}$}
  \Let{interp\_frame}{\textsc{AllocateFrame}(\text{deopt\_info.method})}
  \State $\text{interp\_frame.pc} \gets \text{deopt\_info.bci}$
  \For{each local variable $l$ in deopt\_info}
    \Let{location}{\text{deopt\_info.local\_map}[l]}
    \State $\text{interp\_frame.local}[l] \gets \text{ReadLocation}(\text{optimized\_frame}, \text{location})$
  \EndFor
  \For{each stack entry $s$ in deopt\_info}
    \Let{location}{\text{deopt\_info.stack\_map}[s]}
    \State $\text{interp\_frame.stack}[s] \gets \text{ReadLocation}(\text{optimized\_frame}, \text{location})$
  \EndFor
  \For{each monitor in deopt\_info}
    \State \textsc{ReenterMonitor}(\text{interp\_frame}, \text{monitor})
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $\text{interp\_frame}$
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Caller Frame Reconstruction}\label{sec:caller-reconstruction}

In some cases, deoptimization must reconstruct not just the current frame but also the caller's frame. This occurs when:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The optimized code inlined the caller, so the caller's frame does not exist.
  \item The optimized code performed tail call optimization, merging the caller and callee frames.
\end{itemize}

In these cases, the deoptimization metadata must describe how to reconstruct the entire chain of inlined frames.

\section{Deoptimization Descriptors}\label{sec:deopt-descriptors}

\subsection{Descriptor Format}\label{sec:descriptor-format}

Deoptimization descriptors must encode:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Location}: where in the optimized code the deoptimization occurs.
  \item \textbf{Action}: what to do (deoptimize to interpreter, deoptimize to a less-optimized tier, recompile).
  \item \textbf{Reason}: why deoptimization occurred (for profiling and debugging).
  \item \textbf{State}: the complete mapping from optimized to unoptimized state.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Encoding Strategies}\label{sec:encoding-strategies}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Inline encoding}: the deoptimization information is embedded in the code stream (e.g., as a trap instruction followed by metadata).
  \item \textbf{Side table}: the deoptimization information is stored in a separate table, indexed by the code address.
  \item \textbf{Compressed encoding}: common patterns (e.g., ``all locals are in callee-saved registers'') are encoded compactly.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Lazy Deoptimization}\label{sec:lazy-deopt}

Some systems use \emph{lazy deoptimization}: instead of deoptimizing immediately when a guard fails, the system marks the code for deoptimization and waits for a safe point (e.g., a method call or loop back-edge) to perform the actual deoptimization.

\begin{definition}[Lazy Deoptimization]\label{def:lazy-deopt}
\emph{Lazy deoptimization} defers the actual frame reconstruction until a safe point, using an immediate handler that simply records the need for deoptimization and continues execution until a safe point is reached.
\end{definition}

This approach simplifies the deoptimization implementation (which can then assume it is at a known safe point) but introduces a delay between the guard failure and the actual deoptimization.

\section{On-Stack Replacement: Entering and Exiting Optimized Code}\label{sec:osr}

\subsection{OSR Entry}\label{sec:osr-entry}

On-stack replacement allows a running method to transition from unoptimized to optimized execution:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The interpreter detects a hot loop (back-edge count exceeds threshold).
  \item The JIT compiles an OSR version of the method, starting at the loop header.
  \item At the next back-edge, the interpreter transfers its state to the OSR entry.
  \item The OSR entry maps the interpreter's state to the optimized code's registers and stack slots.
  \item Execution continues in the optimized code.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{OSR State Mapping}\label{sec:osr-state-mapping}

The OSR entry must map the interpreter's state (local variables, operand stack) to the optimized code's expectations. This is more constrained than deoptimization because:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The optimized code may have eliminated some variables (dead code elimination).
  \item The optimized code may have introduced new variables (scalar replacement, loop-invariant code motion).
  \item The types expected by the optimized code may not match the interpreter's types.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{OSR Exit (Deoptimization from OSR Code)}\label{sec:osr-exit}

OSR-compiled code may also need to deoptimize:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A speculation made during OSR compilation is violated.
  \item The method is no longer hot (e.g., the loop exits).
  \item A higher-tier compilation is available.
\end{itemize}

OSR exit is essentially the same as regular deoptimization, but the target is the interpreter frame that would have existed if OSR had never occurred.

\subsection{Multiple OSR Entries}\label{sec:multiple-osr}

A method with multiple loops may have multiple OSR entry points, one for each loop. The interpreter selects the appropriate entry based on which loop is hot.

\section{Debugging Challenges}\label{sec:debugging}

\subsection{The Debugging Problem}\label{sec:debug-problem}

Deoptimization and OSR complicate debugging because:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The debugger sees optimized code, but the programmer expects to debug the source code.
  \item Variables may be optimized away or stored in unexpected locations.
  \item Stepping through code may trigger deoptimization, changing the execution path.
  \item Breakpoints may need to force deoptimization to ensure correct behavior.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Debug Info in Optimized Code}\label{sec:debug-info}

Modern JITs maintain debug information for optimized code:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Debug info records} map each machine instruction to its source location and variable locations.
  \item \emph{Deoptimization metadata} doubles as debug info: it describes how to reconstruct the source-level state at each point.
  \item \emph{Debug scopes} track inlined method boundaries, enabling the debugger to show the correct call stack.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Breakpoint Implementation}\label{sec:breakpoints}

Breakpoints in JIT-compiled code require:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Patching the code at the breakpoint location with a trap instruction.
  \item When the trap fires, deoptimizing the current frame (if in optimized code).
  \item Transferring control to the debugger.
  \item When resuming, re-optimizing or continuing in the interpreter.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Modern Approaches}\label{sec:modern-approaches}

\subsection{Deoptless}\label{sec:deoptless}

\begin{definition}[Deoptless]\label{def:deoptless}
\emph{Deoptless} is an approach that eliminates deoptimization entirely by compiling both the optimistic and pessimistic paths, using predication or conditional execution to select between them at runtime.
\end{definition}

Instead of deoptimizing when a guard fails, the code transitions to a pre-compiled fallback path. This eliminates the cost of frame reconstruction but increases code size.

\subsection{Speculative OSR}\label{sec:speculative-osr}

\emph{Speculative OSR} extends OSR with speculation: the OSR entry not only maps the interpreter state but also makes speculative assumptions about types and values. If the assumptions hold, the optimized code runs at full speed. If not, a different OSR entry (or the interpreter) handles the general case.

\subsection{Eager Deoptimization}\label{sec:eager-deopt}

Some systems use \emph{eager deoptimization}: when an assumption is globally invalidated (e.g., a new class is loaded), all dependent compiled code is immediately deoptimized, rather than waiting for a guard to fail. This approach:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simplifies the code (no guards needed for global assumptions).
  \item Reduces the number of deoptimization points.
  \item May cause unnecessary deoptimization (code that would have been correct is deoptimized).
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced Frame Reconstruction}\label{sec:advanced-frame-recon}

\subsection{Reconstructing Inlined Frames}\label{sec:reconstruct-inlined}

When the JIT inlines method $B$ into method $A$, the frame for $B$ does not exist at runtime. Deoptimization must reconstruct both $A$'s frame and $B$'s frame from $A$'s compiled frame:

\begin{definition}[Inlined Frame Reconstruction]\label{def:inlined-recon}
\emph{Inlined frame reconstruction} is the process of creating multiple interpreter frames from a single compiled frame, where each created frame corresponds to a method that was inlined into the compiled code.
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Source code:
  ;   A() { x = B(y); z = C(x); }
  ;   B(y) { return y * 2; }
  ;   C(x) { return x + 1; }
  ;
  ; After inlining B and C into A:
  ;   A_compiled() { x = y * 2; z = x + 1; }
  ;
  ; Deopt metadata at the point after C's inlined body:
  ;   Frame A: pc = bci_of_C_call, locals = {y -> RAX}
  ;   Frame B: pc = bci_of_return, locals = {y -> RAX}, return_val -> RBX
  ;   Frame C: pc = bci_of_return, locals = {x -> RBX}, return_val -> RCX
\end{verbatim}

The reconstruction creates frames bottom-up: first $C$'s frame (the innermost inlined method), then $B$'s frame, then $A$'s frame.

\subsection{Materializing Virtual Objects}\label{sec:materialize-objects}

Escape analysis may have determined that an object does not escape the method and replaced it with scalar values (scalar replacement). On deoptimization, the object must be \emph{materialized}: reconstructed from its scalar components.

\begin{definition}[Object Materialization]\label{def:object-materialization}
\emph{Object materialization} is the process of creating a heap-allocated object from scalar values during deoptimization, reversing the scalar replacement optimization.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Object Materialization During Deoptimization}
\label{alg:materialization}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{MaterializeObject}{\text{deopt\_info}, \text{obj\_desc}}
  \Let{obj}{\textsc{Allocate}(obj\_desc.class)}
  \For{each field $f$ in obj\_desc}
    \Let{location}{\text{obj\_desc.field\_map}[f]}
    \Let{value}{\textsc{ReadLocation}(\text{optimized\_frame}, \text{location})}
    \State \textsc{SetField}(obj, f, value)
  \EndFor
  \State \Return obj
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Materialization can be recursive: a scalar-replaced object may contain references to other scalar-replaced objects, all of which must be materialized.

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Materializing Cycles Without Losing Object Identity}

Recursively materializing a field as soon as it is read can loop on a cycle or allocate multiple physical objects for one virtual identity. Both behaviors violate deoptimization correctness. Allocate a shell for each identity before filling any virtual references, then read all aliases through the same mapping.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} The descriptor contains a finite, transitively closed set of live virtual identities with class and field recipes. The outgoing frame and any existing reference recipes remain visible to the collector. Objects permit zeroed shells that the collector can safely scan. Allocating a shell and registering its temporary root must not leave an unrooted reference across a safepoint; field writes use the required barriers.

\begin{sudocode}[Allocate shells, then wire their fields]
\Require Virtual descriptors $D$, frame recipes $F$
\Ensure Rebuilt frame graph preserves virtual identity
\State Validate the descriptors before changing frames
\ForAll{virtual IDs $i$ in $D$}
  \Let{H[i]}{\Call{AllocateRootedShell}{\mathrm{class}(i)}}
  \State \Comment{One rooted handle per virtual identity}
\EndFor
\ForAll{$i$ in $D$ and fields $f$ of $i$}
  \Let{v}{\Call{Resolve}{D[i,f],H}}
  \State Store $v$ into field $f$ through $H[i]$
  \State \Comment{References use shells; no recursive allocation}
\EndFor
\State Rebuild frames from $F$ using $H$
\State Publish frames as roots before dropping $H$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} A descriptor has $A.\mathrm{next}=B$, $B.\mathrm{next}=A$, and $B.\mathrm{count}=7$. Two frame slots both name $A$. Allocate two objects, then set both links and the integer field. The two slots compare equal by identity, and following \texttt{next} twice returns to the original object. Allocating independently per frame slot would break the identity comparison.

\textbf{Invariant.} Every populated reference to a virtual object points to the unique shell for its recorded target. The work is linear in identities, field recipes, and recovered frame slots. In a moving collector, stores and frame reconstruction must dereference the updated handles instead of caching a raw address across a safepoint. An allocation failure must not resume with partially reconstructed frames; the runtime needs an explicit failure policy before it attempts publication.

\subsection{Eliminating Redundant Materializations}\label{sec:elim-materialization}

If the same virtual object is referenced from multiple deoptimization points, the system should materialize it only once and share the result:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Identity tracking}: the deopt metadata records which scalar values correspond to the same virtual object.
  \item \textbf{Lazy materialization}: the object is materialized only when first needed; subsequent references use the already-materialized object.
  \item \textbf{Partial materialization}: if only some fields of the virtual object are needed, only those fields are materialized.
\end{itemize}

\section{Modern Deoptimization Approaches}\label{sec:modern-deopt-approaches}

\subsection{Patch-Based Deoptimization}\label{sec:patch-deopt}

Instead of using explicit guard instructions, some systems use \emph{code patching}: the code at a potential deoptimization point initially contains a jump to the optimized path, and when deoptimization is needed, the jump target is patched to redirect to the deoptimization handler.

\begin{definition}[Patch-Based Deoptimization]\label{def:patch-deopt}
\emph{Patch-based deoptimization} replaces explicit guard instructions with patchable code locations. When an assumption is globally invalidated, the code at each dependent location is patched to redirect execution to the deoptimization handler.
\end{definition}

Advantages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item No runtime cost for the guard (just a normal jump).
  \item Deoptimization can be triggered without the code executing a guard.
  \item Simpler code (no explicit deopt branches).
\end{itemize}

Disadvantages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Requires writable and executable memory (or dual-mapped code pages).
  \item Patching must be thread-safe (other threads may be executing the code).
  \item Instruction cache synchronization is required after patching.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Continuation-Based Deoptimization}\label{sec:continuation-deopt}

Some modern systems use \emph{continuations} to implement deoptimization:

\begin{definition}[Continuation-Based Deoptimization]\label{def:continuation-deopt}
\emph{Continuation-based deoptimization} captures the execution state as a continuation (a first-class representation of the control state) and resumes it in the unoptimized code.
\end{definition}

This approach:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simplifies frame reconstruction (the continuation already contains the state).
  \item Enables deoptimization across multiple stack frames naturally.
  \item Is well-suited to languages with first-class continuations (Scheme, Kotlin coroutines).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Bailout Without Full Reconstruction}\label{sec:partial-bailout}

Full deoptimization (reconstructing all interpreter frames) is expensive. Some systems support \emph{partial bailout}: instead of deoptimizing to the interpreter, the code transitions to a less-optimized compiled version that still runs at reasonable speed.

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Full deopt chain:
  ;   Tier 4 (aggressive) -> Tier 3 (optimizing) -> Tier 2 (profiling)
  ;                        -> Tier 1 (baseline) -> Tier 0 (interpreter)
  ;
  ; Partial bailout:
  ;   Tier 4 -> Tier 3 (skip interpreter entirely)
  ;   This is faster because Tier 3 is still compiled code.
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Speculative Inlining and Deoptimization}\label{sec:spec-inline-deopt}

Speculative inlining---inlining a method based on the assumption that it has only one implementation---requires careful deoptimization handling:

\begin{itemize}
  \item When the assumption is violated (a new implementation is loaded), all inlined call sites must be deoptimized.
  \item The deoptimization must reconstruct the callee's frame as if the call had been made dynamically.
  \item The return address must be adjusted to point to the correct location in the unoptimized caller.
\end{itemize}

\section{OSR: Bidirectional Transitions}\label{sec:osr-bidirectional}

\subsection{OSR Loop Transitions}\label{sec:osr-loop-transitions}

A long-running loop may undergo multiple OSR transitions:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The interpreter executes the loop; the back-edge count triggers tier 1 compilation.
  \item The tier 1 OSR entry maps interpreter state to compiled state.
  \item The tier 1 code runs; profiling reveals the loop is very hot, triggering tier 4 compilation.
  \item The tier 4 OSR entry maps tier 1 state to tier 4 state.
  \item The loop exits; the tier 4 code returns to the caller.
\end{enumerate}

Each transition requires a state mapping from the source tier's representation to the target tier's representation.

The entry and exit maps need not be inverses at the machine level:
\Cref{fig:visual-94} shows an illustrative counter loop that modifies
both live values between the two transitions.

