\subsection{OSR and Exception Handling}\label{sec:osr-exception-handling}

When OSR occurs inside a try-catch block, the compiled code must have correct exception handler information:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The exception table for the OSR-compiled code must cover the loop body.
  \item If an exception occurs in the OSR code, the handler must be the same one that would have been used by the interpreter.
  \item This may require the OSR code to include exception handler metadata that maps back to the interpreter's handler chain.
\end{itemize}

\section{Performance Engineering for Deoptimization}\label{sec:deopt-perf}

\subsection{Measuring Deoptimization Impact}\label{sec:measuring-deopt}

Modern JITs provide detailed deoptimization diagnostics:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Deoptimization counters}: track how many times each method deoptimizes and for what reason.
  \item \textbf{Deoptimization reasons}: classify each deoptimization by its cause (type check, null check, class hierarchy, etc.).
  \item \textbf{Recompilation limits}: if a method deoptimizes too many times, the JIT stops recompiling it and falls back to the interpreter permanently.
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Common deoptimization reasons and frequencies}
\label{tab:deopt-reasons}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Reason} & \textbf{Frequency} & \textbf{Mitigation} \\
\hline
Type check failure & Common & Widen speculation \\
Null check failure & Rare & Add null check before guard \\
Class hierarchy change & Rare & Version assumptions \\
Overflow & Common & Use wider type or bail out \\
Uninitialized field & Rare & Delay speculation \\
Array length change & Very rare & Guard on array identity \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Avoiding Deoptimization Pathologies}\label{sec:avoiding-pathologies}

Several strategies help avoid deoptimization pathologies:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Conservative speculation}: for code that is known to be polymorphic, do not speculate.
  \item \textbf{Profile confidence}: only speculate when the profile has high confidence (many samples, stable distribution).
  \item \textbf{Speculation width}: when a speculation fails, widen it (e.g., from ``type is String'' to ``type is String or StringBuilder'') rather than abandoning speculation entirely.
  \item \textbf{Compilation throttling}: if too many methods are deoptimizing simultaneously, slow down compilation to avoid wasting resources.
\end{itemize}

\section{Deoptimization and Garbage Collection}\label{sec:deopt-gc}

\subsection{The Interaction}\label{sec:deopt-gc-interaction}

Deoptimization and garbage collection interact in subtle ways. During deoptimization, new objects may need to be allocated (for materialized virtual objects), but the garbage collector may be in the middle of a collection cycle.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Safe points}: deoptimization can only occur at safe points where the GC state is consistent.
  \item \textbf{Object maps}: materialized objects must have correct type maps so the GC can trace them.
  \item \textbf{Root scanning}: the deoptimization runtime must correctly identify all roots (including newly materialized objects).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Write Barriers During Deoptimization}\label{sec:write-barriers}

If the garbage collector uses write barriers (e.g., for generational or concurrent collection), the deoptimization runtime must respect them when writing field values into materialized objects:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; During object materialization:
  obj.field[i] = value;
  ; Must invoke write barrier:
  write_barrier(obj, &obj.field[i], value);
  ; This ensures the GC tracks the new reference correctly.
\end{verbatim}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:deopt-summary}

Deoptimization and on-stack replacement are the mechanisms that make speculative optimization safe. They provide the bidirectional bridge between optimized and unoptimized execution, enabling the compiler to make aggressive assumptions while maintaining correctness. The key challenges are frame reconstruction (mapping optimized state back to unoptimized state), handling inlined frames, managing the interaction with debugging, and minimizing the overhead of deoptimization itself.

\begin{historical}
On-stack replacement was pioneered by the Self VM (1994), which needed it to optimize methods that were already running (since Self had no explicit ``call'' that would trigger compilation). The concept was adopted by Java's HotSpot VM and has since become standard in all serious JIT compilers. Deoptimization was formalized in the context of the Java HotSpot compiler's uncommon trap mechanism. Modern systems like GraalVM and V8 have pushed the sophistication of deoptimization and OSR, supporting complex scenarios like deoptimizing through multiple inlined frames, handling OSR with speculative types, and maintaining debug information through all transitions.
\end{historical}
